areas, the Eastern San Juan Mountains, and the Upper Rio\nGrande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 AM MST Sunday through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone location.\nRockslides will be possible in the mountainous areas. Highway 160\nand Highway 17 will likely be significantly impacted by this storm.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Heavy rainfall is expected to fall Sunday and Monday across the\narea, leading to elevated concerns for flooding in areas that\nhave recently received excessive moisture, or have recently\nexperienced flooding.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe South Fork of the Rio Grande, including South Fork and\nMogote.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood http://cotrip.org","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.928785af67df25edfd60d6c08af371547045920b.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.928785af67df25edfd60d6c08af371547045920b.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above tr